{
  "borough": "London Borough of Barking and Dagenham",
  "application_type": "All Other",
  "decision": "Refused",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development would result in the loss of two family-sized dwellinghouses, the need for which is clearly demonstrated in the development plan and supporting evidence base. While it would provide a dwelling in the form of an HMO, it fails to demonstrate that this meets an identified need, and further would cause a loss of local amenity including to neighbouring occupiers. Additionally, the development site is not located within an area of high access to public transport and is therefore not considered an appropriate location for an HMO. As such, the proposal is considered to have a detrimental impact on the borough&#39;s housing supply and is contrary to the following policies:&nbsp;National Planning Policy Framework (MHCLG, December 2024)Policies&nbsp;D6, D14 and T4&nbsp;of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies SP3, SP8, DMD1, DMT1, DMH5 and DMSI3&nbsp;of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan&nbsp;(September 2024)",
    "The proposed development fails to demonstrate&nbsp;that additional demand for parking from the development could be adequately accommodated in the surrounding area. The proposal is therefore likely to add unacceptably to parking stress and congestion in the vicinity of the application site.&nbsp; As such, it fails to comply with the following policies&nbsp;which seek to ensure proposals show how car parking will be designed and managed and to take into consideration on-street parking availability and existing and planned public transport provision: National Planning Policy Framework (MHCLG, December 2024) Policies T1, T6 and T6.1 of the London Plan (March 2021)Policies DMT1 and&nbsp;DMT2 of the Barking and Dagenham Local Plan (September 2024)",
    "The proposed hipped roof extension, by extending further forward than the unified ridge line of the terrace row, would result in a visually jarring feature. This design would disrupt the cohesive character of the area and compromise the uniformity of the terrace row, leading to a fragmented and unbalanced appearance, notable given the sites location within the Becontree Estate. As such, the proposed development is considered to be contrary to:National Planning Policy Framework (MHCLG, December 2024)Policies D4 and HC1 of the London Plan (March, 2021)Policies SP2, SPP6, DMD1, and DMD4 of the LBBD Local Plan (September 2024)"
  ],
